labor:
Clasping by faith our Saviour's hand;
To learn to labor, live and fight
On the side of God and changeless light.

labours:
At morn to wake more heav'nly, more refin'd;
So shall the labours of the day begin
More pure, more guarded from the snares of sin.

Lacking the subtler music in his measure,
With useless care labours but to be spurned,
Courting in alien speech the Muse’s pleasure;

He cried, ‘True. After Adam, work was curse;
The natural creature labours, sweats and frets.
But, after Christ, work turns to privilege;

labour:
You’re poor, except in what you richly give;
You labour for your own bread painfully,
Or ere you pour our wine. For art’s sake, pause.’

Light labour more, and his foot would stand
On the threshold, all labour done;
Easy pleasure laid at his hand,

laboring:
So near the freedom and rush of the sea?
“I have listened all night to its laboring sound,
It heaves and sags, as the moon runs round;

labored:
How long at that celestial wick
The angels labored diligent;
Extinguished, now, for you!

labors:
To uphold it by their might;
None were stronger in the labors,
None were braver in the fight.

labouh:
Oh, de ol' plantation 's callin' to me, Come, come back,
Hyeah 's de place fu' you to labouh an' to res',
'Fu my sandy roads is gleamin' w'ile de city ways is black;

labyrinth:
With maiden coy caprice
In a labyrinth of throbs,
Pauses, cadences;
